  "worked_solution": "Let $U$ be the event that the chosen pen is blue, and let $A$ be the event that the pen was selected from drawer $A.$ Then, ${A}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the pen was selected from drawer $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected pen is blue, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(U)=P(U|A)P(A)+P(U|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{4}{5}$ of the pens are in drawer $A$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ are in drawer $B$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{4}{5},\\,P({A}^{\u2032})=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the pens in drawer $A$ are blue and $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the pens in drawer $B$ are blue, we have\n\n$$\nP(U|A)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P(U|{A}^{\u2032})=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected pen is blue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(U) & =P(U|A)P(A)+P(U|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3})(\\frac{4}{5})+(\\frac{1}{6})(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}+\\frac{1}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{8+1}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
